WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.550
Hello and welcome to this unusual AI Coffee
Break!

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:11.690
Today we’ll share with you some of our own
work, which was accepted at ACL 2022.

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:17.859
I know what you could think: It might not
seem like much, especially when we compare

00:00:17.859 --> 00:00:24.439
our work with papers that we have previously
covered on the channel, coming from big companies

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:30.090
and many authors, but you know, we are proud
of our paper, it is honest work!

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:36.650
We’ll be presenting this at ACL 2022 in
the last week of May, so in case you will

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:41.450
be attending the ACL conference too, I guess
we’ll see you in Dublin!

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:44.760
Don’t hesitate to say hi if you’re around!

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:50.740
Otherwise, you can have a look at what we
have been working on recently, in this video.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:51.740
Enjoy!

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:52.740
Hello!

00:00:52.740 --> 00:01:00.350
We are happy to present our work on „VALSE”,
a Task-independent benchmark meant for testing

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:05.820
Vision and Language models with specific linguistic
phenomena.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.150
Why did we do the trouble in setting up this
benchmark?

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:16.930
Well, during the last years, we have seen
an explosion of transformer-based vision and

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:21.790
language models pretrained on large amounts
of image-text pairs.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:28.510
Each one of these models pushes state-of-the
art on vision and language tasks, such as

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:34.460
Visual Question Answering, Visual Commonsense
Reasoning, Image Retrieval, Phrase Grounding.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:40.780
So, we got the message, the accuracies on
these task-specific benchmarks are increasing

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:47.810
steadily, but do we know what the models have
actually learned?

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:54.010
What is it that a vision and language transformer
“understood” when assigning a high score

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:58.560
for this image and this sentence to match?

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.590
And a low score for this one?

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.799
Do VL models focus on the right thing?

00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:10.450
Or do they focus on biases as shown by previous
work?

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:18.630
To shed more light on this aspect, we propose
a more task-agnostic direction and introduce

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:26.870
VALSE that tests the sensitivity of VL models
to specific linguistic phenomena that affect

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:31.330
both the linguistic and the visual modalities.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:38.640
We target existence, plurality, counting,
spatial relations, actions, and entity coreference.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:44.239
But how do we test whether the VL models have
captured these phenomena?

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:52.790
By foiling, a method previously applied for
VL models only for noun phrases by Ravi Shekhar

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:58.150
and collaborators, and on counting, by us
in previous work.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:04.870
Foiling basically means that we take the caption
of an image and produce a foil by altering

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:10.020
the caption such that it does not describe
the image anymore.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:18.030
And we do these phrase alterations by focusing
on 6 specific pieces, such as existence, plurality,

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:24.860
counting, spatial relations, actions, and
entity coreference, where each piece can consist

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:33.200
of one or more instruments, in case we found
more than one way to create foil instances.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:40.010
For example, in the case of the actions piece,
we have two instruments: one in which the

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:46.510
action verb is changed with a different action
and one in which actants are swapped.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:51.370
Counting and coreference are also pieces that
have more than one instrument.

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:56.739
We create these foils by making sure that
they fail to describe the image, that they

00:03:56.739 --> 00:04:00.390
are grammatical and otherwise valid sentences.

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:09.420
This is not easy to do because a foiled caption
may be less likely than the original caption:

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:14.970
For example, though not impossible, it is
statistically less likely for plants to cut

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:22.090
a man than a man to cut plants and large VL
models could pick up on this.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:29.320
Therefore, to obtain valid foils, we must
take action: First, we make use of strong

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:32.020
language models to propose foils.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:40.320
Second, we use Natural Language Inference
to filter out foils that could be still describing

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:45.419
the image, since when constructing foils,
we need to ensure that they fail to describe

00:04:45.419 --> 00:04:46.419
the image.

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:51.080
To test this automatically, we apply natural
language inference (NLI) with the following

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:58.370
rationale: We consider an image to be the
premise and its caption its entailed hypothesis.

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:05.160
In addition, we consider the caption to be
the premise and the foil is its hypothesis.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:12.100
If an NLI model predicts the foil to contradict
or to be neutral with respect to the caption,

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:16.340
we take this as an indicator of a valid foil.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:22.830
If an NLI model predicts the foil to be entailed
by the caption, it cannot be a good foil since

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:30.040
by transitivity it will give a truthful description
of the image and we filter these foils out.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:35.940
But this procedure is not perfect, it is just
an indicator for valid foils, therefore as

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:41.680
a third measure for generating valid foils,
we employ human annotators to validate the

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:44.020
data used in VALSE.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:50.979
So, after filtering and human evaluation,
we have as many test instances as described

00:05:50.979 --> 00:05:52.990
in this table.

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:59.830
Note that VALSE does not deliver any training
data, but only test data since it is a zero-shot

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:02.120
testing benchmark only.

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:10.199
It is designed to leverage the existing capabilities
of VL models after pre-training.

00:06:10.199 --> 00:06:15.490
Fine-tuning would only enable models to exploit
artefacts or statistical biases in the data.

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:20.889
We all know that these models like to cheat
and take shortcuts and as we said, we are

00:06:20.889 --> 00:06:28.199
interested in assessing what capabilities
the VL models have after pre-training.

00:06:28.199 --> 00:06:35.419
We experiment with five VL models on VALSE,
namely with CLIP, LXMERT, ViLBERT, ViLBERT

00:06:35.419 --> 00:06:37.890
12-in-1 and VisualBERT.

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:44.389
Two of our most important evaluation metrics
are: the accuracy of the models in classifying

00:06:44.389 --> 00:06:48.610
image-sentence pairs into captions and foils.

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:54.449
Perhaps more relevant for this video, we will
showcase our more permissive metric, the pairwise

00:06:54.449 --> 00:07:01.729
accuracy which measures whether the image-sentence
alignment score is greater for a correct image-text

00:07:01.729 --> 00:07:04.900
pair than for its foiled pair.

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:08.780
For more metrics, do check out the paper.

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:13.530
The results with pairwise accuracy are shown
here and they are consistent with the results

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:16.150
we got with the other metrics.

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:21.300
The best zero-shot performance is achieved
by ViLBERT 12-in-1, followed by ViLBERT, LXMERT,

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:25.009
CLIP and finally VisualBERT.

00:07:25.009 --> 00:07:31.389
It's notable how instruments centered on individual
objects like existence and noun phrases are

00:07:31.389 --> 00:07:38.090
almost solved by ViLBERT 12-in-1, highlighting
that models are capable of identifying named

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:40.620
objects and their presence in images.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:46.289
However, none of the remaining pieces can
be reliably solved in our adversarial foiling

00:07:46.289 --> 00:07:47.330
settings.

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:53.410
We see from the plurality and counting instruments
that VL models have trouble distinguishing

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:59.080
references to single vs. multiple objects
or counting them in an image.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:04.780
The relation piece shows that they have difficulties
in correctly classifying a named spatial relation

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:07.360
between objects in an image.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:12.819
They also have trouble distinguishing actions
and identifying their participants, even if

00:08:12.819 --> 00:08:18.020
supported by plausibility biases, as we see
in the actions piece.

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:23.130
From the coreference piece we find out that
tracing multiple references to the same object

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:28.860
in an image by using pronouns is also difficult
for VL models.

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:33.479
As a sanity check and also because it is an
interesting experiment, we also benchmark

00:08:33.479 --> 00:08:40.719
two text-only models: GPT1 and GPT2 to assess
whether VALSE is solvable by these unimodal

00:08:40.719 --> 00:08:47.829
models by computing the perplexity of the
correct and foiled caption and predicting

00:08:47.829 --> 00:08:51.189
the entry with the lowest perplexity.

00:08:51.189 --> 00:08:56.689
If the perplexity is higher for the foil,
we take this as an indication that the foiled

00:08:56.689 --> 00:09:01.620
caption may suffer from plausibility bias
or other linguistic biases.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:07.720
It is interesting to see, that in some cases,
the text-only GPT models have captured the

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:11.939
plausibility of the world better than the
vision and language models.

00:09:11.939 --> 00:09:19.069
To sum up, VALSE is a benchmark that uses
the lens of linguistic constructs to help

00:09:19.069 --> 00:09:26.600
the community improve V&amp;L models by hard testing
their visual grounding capabilities.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:32.209
Our experiments show that V&amp;L models identify
named objects and their presence in images

00:09:32.209 --> 00:09:38.259
well (as shown by the existence piece), but
struggle to ground their interdependence and

00:09:38.259 --> 00:09:43.639
relationships in visual scenes when forced
to respect linguistic indicators.

00:09:43.639 --> 00:09:48.600
We would really like to encourage the community
to use VALSE for measuring progress towards

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:52.699
language grounding with V&amp;L models.

00:09:52.699 --> 00:09:57.850
Even more, VALSE could be used as an indirect
assessment of datasets, as models could be

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:04.959
evaluated before and after training or fine-tuning
to see if a dataset helps models improve on

00:10:04.959 --> 00:10:08.509
any of the aspects tested by VALSE.

00:10:08.509 --> 00:10:13.009
If you’re interested, do check out the VALSE
data on GitHub and if you have any questions,

00:10:13.009 --> 00:10:28.019
do not hesitate to contact us!

